      <h2 id="intro">Shoe Polishing in Nairobi Done by Hand, by a Craftsman With a Track Record</h2>

      <p>Shoe polishing is the cheapest way to change how a pair of leather shoes looks and how long it lasts. Done properly, a polish is not a wipe with a cloth. It is a sequence of cleaning, conditioning, layering product, brushing between layers, dressing the edges and buffing to a finish. Done badly, a polish is a smear of grease that attracts dust, darkens the stitching and leaves the leather feeling sticky. The difference between the two is a few minutes of care and the right products for the leather type.</p>

      <p>At <a href="/">FitFix Alterations and Leather Repair</a>, shoe polishing is led by David Ochieng, a leather craftsman with fourteen years of hands on experience. David learned the trade working at Manix Clothing Store on City Walk and then at Italian Mens Wear, both in Nairobi. That background matters for polishing because different leathers take different products. Smooth calfskin wants cream or wax. Suede and nubuck want a brush and a protector, never wax. Patent leather wants a specific patent finish. Shell cordovan wants a whole different routine. Getting the product wrong is what turns a polish into damage.</p>

      <div class="craft">
        <h3>Meet David Ochieng, Master Leather Craftsman</h3>
        <p class="who">Founder of the FitFix leather division. Fourteen years repairing shoes, boots, jackets and bags in Nairobi.</p>
        <p>David Ochieng has spent his working life around leather and menswear in Nairobi. His early years were at <strong>Manix Clothing Store on City Walk</strong>, where he handled footwear and leather goods for some of the most demanding menswear clients in the city. He then moved to <strong>Italian Mens Wear</strong>, where he worked with imported Italian leather shoes and the higher standard of finish those shoes demand.</p>
        <p>Over fourteen years he has polished, conditioned, resoled, re heeled, re stitched, dyed and restored thousands of pairs of leather shoes and boots. He has worked on welted Oxfords, cemented Derbies, Italian leather loafers, Chelsea boots, work boots, safety boots, women's pumps and patina finished dress shoes. He has also handled delicate and expensive pieces where the wrong product would have ruined the finish, which is where his careful approach comes from.</p>
        <blockquote>"A polish tells the shoe how to behave for the next six months. Feed it correctly and the leather stays soft and holds colour. Cover it in the wrong product and it dries out and cracks. That is why I look at the leather first before I open a single tin."</blockquote>
        <p>Every pair that arrives at FitFix for polishing is assessed by David or a craftsman trained directly under him. When you send a photo to 0705728896, you are sending it to a workshop where the person doing the work has spent more than a decade on exactly this trade.</p>
      </div>

      <a class="banner" href="/leather-repair/shoe-repair/color-restoration-and-dyeing/">
        <strong>Colour Restoration and Dyeing in Nairobi</strong>
        <span>Faded black shoes brought back to deep black, patchy brown shoes evened out, scuffed toes recovered and colour matched to the original using penetrating dyes. From KES 1,200.</span>
        <em>See colour restoration and dyeing &rarr;</em>
