      <h3>What a Proper Shoe Polish Actually Involves</h3>

      <p>People imagine a polish as a quick wipe with a cloth and a dab of product. In a proper workshop it is a sequence of at least eight steps, each of which changes the outcome of the finished shoe.</p>

      <ol class="list">
        <li><strong>Assessment.</strong> David identifies the leather type. Smooth calfskin, suede, nubuck, patent, shell cordovan, exotic leathers and corrected grain all take different products and different techniques.</li>
        <li><strong>Dry brushing.</strong> Loose dust and grit are removed with a horsehair brush before any product goes on. Skipping this step pushes grit into the leather and scratches the surface.</li>
        <li><strong>Cleaning.</strong> Dirt, old polish build up and residue are removed with a leather cleaner matched to the leather type. Old polish build up is the single biggest reason shoes look dull even after polishing.</li>
        <li><strong>Conditioning.</strong> A leather conditioner is applied and allowed to soak in. This replaces the oils that Nairobi sun and dry weather strip out and keeps the leather supple rather than brittle.</li>
        <li><strong>Cream or wax application.</strong> Cream polish feeds and adds colour. Wax polish adds shine and surface protection. Most shoes get cream first, wax second. Only wax is used on shell cordovan and a few other finishes.</li>
        <li><strong>Brushing between layers.</strong> Each layer of product is brushed before the next is applied. This is what builds depth and shine rather than a flat greasy coat.</li>
        <li><strong>Edge dressing.</strong> The welt, the sole edge and the heel edges are dressed with the correct colour product so they look clean and finished rather than raw and dark from wear.</li>
        <li><strong>Final buff and inspection.</strong> A horsehair buff brings up the shine, and the pair is checked for missed areas, uneven patches and residue before it goes back to you.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>Each of those steps is what separates a KES 300 polish that lasts a week from a rushed job that looks tired within two days. The polish is not the product. It is the routine.</p>

      <h3>Why Regular Polishing Saves You Money</h3>

      <p>A polish every four to six weeks is the cheapest insurance you can buy for a pair of leather shoes. Here is what regular polishing actually prevents.</p>

      <h4>Prevents Cracking</h4>
      <p>Dry leather cracks. Once cracks form across the vamp or the flex point, the repair moves from a polish at KES 300 into a colour restoration or crack repair at KES 1,500 or more. Conditioning during a polish keeps the leather supple enough that it flexes without splitting.</p>

      <h4>Prevents Colour Fade</h4>
      <p>Nairobi sun bleaches leather over time. Black shoes go grey, brown shoes go patchy. A regular polish with the correct colour cream or wax keeps the surface pigment topped up and prevents the fade from settling in. Once fade has reached the depth of the hide, only a full redye will fix it.</p>

      <h4>Prevents Water Damage</h4>
      <p>Wax polish and protective finishes form a barrier that keeps rainwater out of the leather and the stitching. Without it, water soaks in, the leather swells, the stitching weakens and the shoe takes days to dry properly. Repeated wetting and slow drying is the leading cause of hardened, brittle leather in Nairobi.</p>

      <h4>Protects the Stitching</h4>
      <p>Polish and conditioner work into the stitching along the seams and keep the thread lubricated. Dry thread frays faster and pulls through the leather when the shoe flexes. This is one of the most overlooked reasons to polish regularly.</p>

      <h4>Keeps the Shoe Looking Expensive</h4>
      <p>There is a direct relationship between a well polished shoe and how it is perceived. A scuffed, dull shoe looks cheap regardless of what it cost. A well polished shoe looks expensive regardless of what it cost. That is the simplest reason to polish regularly and the reason most people do it.</p>

