      <h3>Leather Types and the Right Product for Each</h3>

      <p>Not every shoe wants the same polish. Using the wrong product on the wrong leather is the fastest way to ruin a good pair. Here is how we treat each leather type.</p>

      <h4>Smooth Calfskin and Box Calf</h4>
      <p>The most common leather on dress shoes and the easiest to work with. Takes cream polish for feeding and colour, then wax polish for shine and protection. Brushed between layers and buffed to a finish. This is the standard routine for Oxfords, Derbies, loafers and dress boots.</p>

      <h4>Suede and Nubuck</h4>
      <p>Never waxed. Ever. Suede and nubuck are cleaned with a suede brush and a suede specific cleaner, then treated with a protector spray. Wax would flatten the nap and leave an irreversible greasy mark. If your suede shoes look flat and shiny, they have probably been waxed by mistake at some point and a suede clean and brush is the only way back.</p>

      <h4>Patent Leather</h4>
      <p>Patent has a lacquered finish that cannot be polished with normal wax or cream. It is cleaned with a soft damp cloth and finished with a patent specific product. Using wax on patent leaves a cloudy film and dulls the mirror finish. We see this damage regularly on women's patent pumps and men's patent dress shoes used for weddings and events.</p>

      <h4>Shell Cordovan</h4>
      <p>Shell cordovan is a specific horsehide leather with a distinctive finish. It takes very little product, brushed more than polished and treated with a cordovan specific cream or wax. Getting the routine wrong on cordovan leaves streaks and dulls the characteristic shine.</p>

      <h4>Exotic Leathers</h4>
      <p>Alligator, crocodile, ostrich and lizard skins all have specific finishes and specific care routines. They take very light conditioning and the correct colour cream matched to the finish. Over polishing strips the natural texture and destroys the character of the skin.</p>

      <h4>Corrected Grain and Bonded Leather</h4>
      <p>Corrected grain and bonded leathers have a plastic top layer that behaves differently from real hide. They take a light cream and a wipe rather than a heavy wax routine. Too much product sits on top of the plastic and goes sticky, which then attracts dust.</p>

