      <h3>Practical Shoe Polishing You Can Do at Home</h3>

      <ul class="list">
        <li><strong>Brush before you polish.</strong> Dust acts like sandpaper inside the leather. Remove it with a soft horsehair brush or cloth before applying any product.</li>
        <li><strong>Use the right product for the leather.</strong> Cream or wax on smooth calfskin. Suede brush and protector on suede. Patent product on patent. Never mix them up.</li>
        <li><strong>Use thin layers, not a single heavy coat.</strong> Polish builds shine layer by layer with brushing between coats. One thick coat just sits on the surface and attracts dust.</li>
        <li><strong>Condition twice a year.</strong> A light application of leather conditioner in January and again in July keeps the fibres supple through the dry and wet seasons.</li>
        <li><strong>Dress the edges.</strong> A small amount of edge dressing on the sole edge, welt and heel edge makes a big visual difference in a few seconds.</li>
        <li><strong>Let the leather dry between layers.</strong> Polishing a wet shoe pushes the product around rather than into the leather. Give each layer a few minutes to set.</li>
        <li><strong>Do not polish wet shoes.</strong> Water on the surface plus polish equals a smear. Let the shoes dry at room temperature first.</li>
        <li><strong>Use a shoe horn.</strong> A shoe horn saves the heel counter from being crushed every time the shoe goes on, and the heel counter takes polish more evenly when it is not creased.</li>
      </ul>

